i915: relax the eDP VESA-backlight capability gate on Nocturne only (9200, nightfall-kernel variant)

The Google Pixel Slate (Nocturne) eDP panel sets brightness over the DPCD AUX
channel but fails check_if_vesa_backlight_possible() in the current tree, so
i915 falls back to native PWM, which does not physically drive the panel:
sysfs brightness updates, the screen never changes, dmesg is clean.

CORRECTION (found by a peer session auditing this patch, 2026-09-30): earlier
revisions of this commit message and the code comment below named
DP_EDP_BACKLIGHT_AUX_ENABLE_CAP as the bit that fails. That's wrong -
check_if_vesa_backlight_possible() in drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/
intel_dp_aux_backlight.c does not reference AUX_ENABLE_CAP at all (that bit is
only read in drm_dp_helper.c, to choose aux_enable vs pwm_funcs for how the
backlight gets *enabled* - i915 already handles aux_enable == false via
pwm_funcs, so AUX-brightness + PWM-enable is a supported combination upstream).
The function actually rejects on one of three DPCD reads: (a) edp_dpcd[2]
lacking DP_EDP_BACKLIGHT_BRIGHTNESS_AUX_SET_CAP, (b) PWMGEN_BIT_COUNT_CAP_MIN
< 1, or (c) PWMGEN_BIT_COUNT_CAP_MAX < CAP_MIN. Which of the three actually
fails on this panel is UNCONFIRMED - needs a DPCD dump of 0x700-0x703 and
0x724/0x725 on real hardware. Kernel <= 6.18 gated
intel_dp_aux_supports_vesa_backlight() on drm_edp_backlight_supported() alone;
7.x added the check_if_vesa_backlight_possible() call that this panel fails.

DIFFERENCE FROM THE pixel-slate 9200: that patch relaxes the gate for every panel
and says so itself ("runs only on the Pixel Slate ... Do NOT apply on the other
variants"). The nightfall-kernel branch is meant to boot on arbitrary PCs, where
an unconditional relaxation could push a panel that needs PWM onto the AUX path
and leave it dark. Here the relaxation applies only when DMI says Google/Nocturne;
every other machine runs upstream's check unchanged. Behaviour on the Slate is
identical to the pixel-slate patch, and check_if_vesa_backlight_possible() keeps
its caller, so it no longer needs __maybe_unused.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_dp_aux_backlight.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_dp_aux_backlight.c
index 266e042e0..d2bd82884 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_dp_aux_backlight.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_dp_aux_backlight.c
@@ -34,6 +34,8 @@
  * for some reason.
  */
 
+#include <linux/dmi.h>
+
 #include <drm/drm_print.h>
 
 #include "intel_backlight.h"
@@ -637,6 +639,18 @@ check_if_vesa_backlight_possible(struct intel_dp *intel_dp)
 	return true;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Google Nocturne (Pixel Slate): the panel sets brightness over the DPCD AUX
+ * channel but fails check_if_vesa_backlight_possible()'s DPCD checks (exactly
+ * which of its three conditions is unconfirmed - see the commit message).
+ * Only this machine gets the relaxed gate; every other panel keeps upstream's.
+ */
+static bool nocturne_aux_backlight_quirk(void)
+{
+	return dmi_match(DMI_SYS_VENDOR, "Google") &&
+	       dmi_match(DMI_PRODUCT_NAME, "Nocturne");
+}
+
 static bool
 intel_dp_aux_supports_vesa_backlight(struct intel_connector *connector)
 {
@@ -654,7 +668,8 @@ intel_dp_aux_supports_vesa_backlight(struct intel_connector *connector)
 	}
 
 	if (drm_edp_backlight_supported(intel_dp->edp_dpcd) &&
-	    check_if_vesa_backlight_possible(intel_dp)) {
+	    (nocturne_aux_backlight_quirk() ||
+	     check_if_vesa_backlight_possible(intel_dp))) {
 		drm_dbg_kms(display->drm,
 			    "[CONNECTOR:%d:%s] AUX Backlight Control Supported!\n",
 			    connector->base.base.id, connector->base.name);
